WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.795
At the print shop's parcel desk, every number on a job sheet carries a unit: 500 sheets, 2,500 grams.

2
00:00:09.795 --> 00:00:13.378
The clerk reads the units before a parcel goes out.

3
00:00:13.378 --> 00:00:17.311
A sheet that says weight: 500 sheets goes straight back.

4
00:00:17.311 --> 00:00:19.911
In the back room, the units are gone.

5
00:00:19.911 --> 00:00:23.704
The scale and the label printer only ever see numbers.

6
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:27.551
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:27.551 --> 00:00:30.595
People say a value class disappears at run time.

8
00:00:30.595 --> 00:00:33.639
But if it disappears, how can it catch anything?

9
00:00:33.639 --> 00:00:35.668
And what is actually left of it?

10
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:43.211
Start with two numbers from the shop's app: the weight of a parcel in grams, and the number of sheets in it.

11
00:00:43.211 --> 00:00:44.059
Both are Long.

12
00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:46.180
And ship takes the weight in grams.

13
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.935
Now call ship with sheets by mistake.

14
00:00:48.935 --> 00:00:50.785
Will the compiler stop you?

15
00:00:50.785 --> 00:00:51.881
Pause and guess.

16
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:55.847
It compiles.

17
00:00:55.847 --> 00:01:02.937
Both are Long, so the compiler can't tell them apart, and ship happily posts a parcel that weighs 500 sheets.

18
00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:05.018
The courier will have questions.

19
00:01:05.018 --> 00:01:08.725
The type said Long, and Long says nothing about the unit.

20
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:14.517
The classic fix is a small wrapper class, Grams, with one number inside.

21
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:15.874
It catches the swap.

22
00:01:15.874 --> 00:01:22.115
But now every weight is an extra object on the heap, the memory where the JVM keeps objects.

23
00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:25.575
One object per number, just to carry a unit around.

24
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.014
Wouldn't it be nice to keep the unit for the compiler, and drop the object at run time?

25
00:01:31.014 --> 00:01:33.771
That's exactly what Kotlin's value class does.

26
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.270
Here it is.

27
00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:40.944
In Java, the closest shape today is a record, a real object created on every new.

28
00:01:40.944 --> 00:01:45.917
In Kotlin, you write @JvmInline value class, with exactly one property.

29
00:01:45.917 --> 00:01:51.380
It can check its value in an init block, and it can have functions, like plus.

30
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.953
Now ship takes Grams.

31
00:01:52.953 --> 00:01:56.755
Pass sheets, and the compiler stops you, with this message.

32
00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.263
Back to the parcel desk.

33
00:01:59.263 --> 00:02:02.256
The unit on the job sheet is the value class.

34
00:02:02.256 --> 00:02:05.715
The clerk who checks it at the desk is the compiler.

35
00:02:05.715 --> 00:02:09.306
The machines in the back room are the JVM at run time.

36
00:02:09.306 --> 00:02:10.969
They only see the number.

37
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.760
So here's the real test.

38
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.798
The demo adds Grams of 150 and Grams of 49, and ships the result.

39
00:02:18.798 --> 00:02:22.441
How many Grams objects get created at run time?

40
00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:23.681
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:27.258
None.

42
00:02:27.258 --> 00:02:34.069
Let's read the bytecode with javap, the JDK tool that shows what a class file contains.

43
00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:39.407
Grams of 150 becomes a plain long, 150, handed to constructor-impl.

44
00:02:39.407 --> 00:02:43.600
That runs your init block and hands back the same long.

45
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:44.515
Same for 49.

46
00:02:44.515 --> 00:02:48.402
Then plus, now a static method that adds two longs.

47
00:02:48.402 --> 00:02:50.842
And ship takes a plain long too.

48
00:02:50.842 --> 00:02:52.900
Not one new Grams anywhere.

49
00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:55.451
And it prints: shipped 199 g.

50
00:02:55.451 --> 00:03:02.405
Try Grams of minus 1, and the init block still does its job: rejected, negative weight.

51
00:03:03.333 --> 00:03:04.740
Two things to notice.

52
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.221
ship got a strange new name, ship, dash, and a hash.

53
00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:10.096
A later section explains it.

54
00:03:10.096 --> 00:03:16.391
And the Grams class still exists, with box-impl, for the moments when a real object is needed.

55
00:03:16.391 --> 00:03:17.596
Keep that in mind.

56
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.915
Now, you might be thinking: why not a type alias?

57
00:03:21.915 --> 00:03:23.866
typealias GramsAlias = Long.

58
00:03:23.866 --> 00:03:26.654
It's shorter, and it reads just as well.

59
00:03:26.654 --> 00:03:28.396
Let's be fair and try it.

60
00:03:28.396 --> 00:03:30.139
Pass sheets to shipAlias.

61
00:03:30.367 --> 00:03:33.702
It compiles, and prints: shipped 500 g, alias.

62
00:03:33.702 --> 00:03:35.660
A type alias is a nickname.

63
00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.923
Calling sheets grams doesn't make them grams.

64
00:03:38.923 --> 00:03:40.953
A value class is a new type.

65
00:03:41.867 --> 00:03:45.603
You already use value classes from the standard library.

66
00:03:45.603 --> 00:03:49.539
UInt, ULong, Duration and Result are all declared this way.

67
00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:52.592
Here's where the picture breaks.

68
00:03:52.592 --> 00:03:57.097
Java has no value classes, so it walks in through a side door.

69
00:03:57.097 --> 00:04:04.436
With @JvmName, Kotlin can give Java a function it can call, shipFromJava, and Java passes a raw long.

70
00:04:04.436 --> 00:04:07.560
Java passed minus 5, and got: shipped -5 g.

71
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.484
It never passed the desk, so the init block never ran.

72
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:15.952
So if Java can call it, check the value again inside the function.

73
00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:18.062
And one more thing.

74
00:04:18.062 --> 00:04:21.587
The number travels loose only while it's used as itself.

75
00:04:21.587 --> 00:04:25.678
Some shelves in the shop only take envelopes, like a shared list.

76
00:04:25.678 --> 00:04:28.510
Put a Grams there, and it goes back in a box.

77
00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.020
That's the next section.

78
00:04:30.933 --> 00:04:32.167
So here's the rule.

79
00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:38.008
When two values share a type but must never be swapped, give each one its own value class.

80
00:04:38.233 --> 00:04:40.922
So, what's left of a value class at run time?

81
00:04:40.922 --> 00:04:41.878
Just the number.

82
00:04:41.878 --> 00:04:47.853
The unit lived at the desk, where the compiler checked it, and that's where the mistake gets caught.

83
00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:48.833
Quick check.

84
00:04:48.833 --> 00:04:53.173
A function takes a page count and a weight, both Long, side by side.

85
00:04:53.173 --> 00:04:56.812
What would you change, and what does it cost at run time?

86
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.413
Give each one its own value class, Sheets and Grams.

87
00:05:03.413 --> 00:05:05.383
Now a swap is a compile error.

88
00:05:05.383 --> 00:05:08.468
And at run time, they're still two plain longs.
